<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cooperative Cyber Defence Centre of Excellence</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2008">2008</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a> · Also known as CCDCOE</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> Cooperative Cyber Defence Centre of Excellence is a research and training centre on cyber defence in Tallinn, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>. Proposed by Estonia and established in 2008, the year after large cyberattacks on Estonian government and banking websites, it supports NATO members with research, exercises and legal analysis, and produced the Tallinn Manual on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-law">international law</a> in cyber warfare.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cooperative-cyber-defence-centre-of-excellence.jpg" alt="Cooperative Cyber Defence Centre of Excellence" width="320" height="321" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Worldlydev · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emblem_of_the_NATO_Cooperative_Cyber_Defence_Centre_of_Excellence.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In 2003, before it formally joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> proposed the creation of a Centre of Excellence. NATO's 2006 Riga summit listed possible cyber attacks among the asymmetric threats to common security, and the cyber attacks on Estonia in 2007 showed for the first time how vulnerable NATO countries, their institutions and NATO itself could be to disruption of their information and communications systems. Estonia's proposal won strong support from NATO Secretary-General Jaap de Hoop Scheffer. NATO completed an assessment in April 2007, approved a policy on cyber defence in January 2008, and announced at its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucharest">Bucharest</a> summit that April its readiness to help allies counter a cyber attack on request.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The centre was established on 14 May 2008; General James Mattis, speaking in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> that day, called the need for it compelling. On 28 October 2008 it received full accreditation from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> and the status of an International Military Organisation. Its founding states were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. Hungary joined in 2010, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 2011 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> in 2012. In 2013 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the United Kingdom and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a> joined, together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, the first non-NATO country.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> followed in November 2015, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> in June 2019 and Ireland in October 2019. Colonel Jaak Tarien succeeded Merle Maigre as director on 30 August 2018. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> applied in August 2021 and was accepted as a contributing participant in March 2022. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a> joined in May 2022 as the first Asian country, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in November 2022. In March 2024 the centre had 32 sponsoring nations, a status open only to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> members, and seven non-NATO contributing participants.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The centre is one of 21 accredited NATO Centres of Excellence, funded nationally and multinationally and closely linked with Allied Command Transformation. Its mission is to improve the capability, cooperation and information sharing of NATO, its member nations and partners in cyber defence through education, research and development, lessons learned and consultation. Its work covers the doctrine and concepts of cyber defence, education and training, support for experimentation, exercises and the legal aspects of cyber defence. It may also establish cooperative relations with non-NATO states, universities, research institutions and businesses.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cooperative Cyber Defence Centre of Excellence” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the cyberattacks of 2007, NATO located its <b>Cooperative Cyber Defence Centre of Excellence</b> in Tallinn, making Estonia a reference point in debates on cyber security.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-estonia">Republic of Estonia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
